  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nCurrently, a cut off low is positioned over California and the \nPacific Coast with a shortwave trough extending into the Southern \nPlains. At the surface, a weak low pressure is situated over north-\ncentral OK leading to areas of increased surface vorticity. \nWidespread showers are impacting much of the forecast area. However, \nnear the center of the surface low, some clearing is allowing the \ndevelopment of isolated thunderstorms. As these thunderstorms move \nthrough the areas of increased surface vorticity, we could see a \nbrief funnel or spinup this afternoon across south-central Kansas. \nThrough the evening, the shortwave will continue to lift into the \nregion and will force the showers/storms north of our area. Spotty \nrain activity will persist for northern and eastern portions of the \nforecast area into Friday morning. Additional widespread rainfall \namounts through early Friday morning will reach between 0.10-0.25\" \nwith localized amounts under the heavier thunderstorms of up to 1 \ninch.\n\nThrough the day Friday, the cutoff low out west will begin to be \ncaught up in the broader flow and will begin to lift across the \nRockies. The shortwave to our south will move over the area by \nFriday afternoon lifting a weak warm frontal boundary from southwest \nto northeast across the state. This will be the focus of potential \nstorm development for Friday afternoon. Sufficient instability and \ndecent 0-3km shear will support a few stronger to marginally severe \nstorms by late afternoon, lasting into the evening hours. \n\nAs we move into Saturday, the western trough will push into the High \nPlains and a surface low will develop in the lee of the Rockies, \nlifting a frontal boundary/dryline across the state on Saturday \nafternoon/evening. Ample instability and deep shear will make the \nmost of a relatively uncapped environment leading to another day of \npotential severe development. Strong winds and large hail would be \nthe primary threats with the strongest storms, though a brief \ntornado or two cannot be ruled out. This activity will quickly move \nnortheast across the area, with the severe threat shifting out of \nthe area by 06Z. \n\nAs the upper level and lee surface trough linger over the High \nPlains into Sunday morning, a few widely scattered showers could \nlinger, especially over northern and eastern portions of the \nforecast area through the morning hours. Then for Sunday afternoon \nand into early next week, a mid/upper level ridge will begin to \nbuild in over the region. This will lead to not only increasing \ntemps (upper 80s, lower 90s), but also a less supportive environment \nfor more potent convection due to subsidence. But given the buoyant \nlower-level airmass, some diurnally driven showers and storms could \ndevelop each afternoon/evening. Confidence remains low on exact \nlocation and intensity of any convection for Sunday afternoon \nthrough Wednesday.",
